SFCM_DATA_PART("nonprofits/insights/insights.json",0,1,{"sample":false,"as_of":"2026-09-26","dashboard":"nonprofits","intro":"These findings about the city's payments to nonprofits were chosen from about thirty candidates we tested against the city's vendor payments, contracts and fund records, the organizations' own IRS Form 990 filings, the Controller's monitoring reports and published outcome measures. Each survived at least three robustness checks (other base years and deflators, excluding organizations that mostly pass money on, alternative definitions and thresholds), and a separate script recomputes every number from the source tables. Dollar figures are city payments to organizations matched to IRS exempt-organization records; real dollars are FY2025 dollars. Unusual is not improper: these are patterns worth discussing, not findings about anyone's conduct.","insights":[{"id":"homelessness-led-the-growth","rank":1,"theme":"spending-growth","title":"Homelessness services drove 40% of the 132% real rise in nonprofit payments","dek":"City payments to nonprofits rose from $810 million (in FY2025 dollars) in FY2016 to $1.88 billion in FY2025. Organizations working on homelessness and housing added $424 million of the $1.07 billion increase; children, youth and families added $243 million and health and behavioral health $182 million.","why_it_matters":"Three service areas account for 79% of the growth, so decisions about homelessness, early childhood and behavioral health budgets largely determine how much the city spends through nonprofits. Arts and culture, by contrast, received no more in real terms than in FY2016.","surprise":"Health was the largest service area in FY2016, but homelessness organizations grew +260% in real terms, 4.4 times the rate of health organizations.","numbers":[{"label":"City payments to nonprofits, real, FY2016 → FY2025","value":"$810 million → $1.88 billion (+132%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator)"},{"label":"Added by homelessness and housing organizations","value":"$424 million (40% of the increase)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator); nonprofits/data/site/orgs/index.json#area"},{"label":"Added by children, youth and families organizations","value":"$243 million (23%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator); nonprofits/data/site/orgs/index.json#area"},{"label":"Added by health and behavioral health organizations","value":"$182 million (17%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator); nonprofits/data/site/orgs/index.json#area"}],"chart":{"type":"waterfall","title":"Where the extra billion went","subtitle":"Change in city payments to nonprofits by the organization's main service area, FY2016 to FY2025, millions of FY2025 dollars","x":{"label":"Service area","values":["FY2016 total","Homelessness and housing","Children, youth and families","Health and behavioral health","Human services and seniors","Workforce and economic development","Other","Arts and culture","FY2025 total"]},"y":{"label":"Millions of FY2025 dollars","unit":"$M"},"series":[{"name":"Payments","values":[809.62,423.91,243.2,182.2,132.08,47.51,42.47,-0.95,1880.03]}],"highlight":"Homelessness and housing","note":"Source: city Vendor Payments (DataSF n9pm-xkyq), expenditure payments to organizations matched to IRS exempt-organization records. Each organization has one service area (its main line of work), so a homelessness nonprofit's health contracts count as homelessness. Real dollars are FY2025 dollars (SF-Oakland-Hayward CPI-U, BLS CUURS49BSA0)."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held from FY2019 instead of FY2016 (after the accounting-system change): homelessness organizations 47% of the growth, the top three areas 87%.","Held to FY2026: homelessness organizations 38% of the growth since FY2016.","Held excluding organizations that mostly pass money on (childcare-subsidy administrators, a loan fund, fiscal sponsors): homelessness 43%, children and families 11%.","Held with other deflators: real growth 134% with the U.S. CPI and 124% with the U.S. services CPI (red-team re-derivation)."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Service areas are assigned per organization, not per contract, so multi-service agencies are counted in one area. Department reorganizations (the homelessness department began in FY2017) do not affect this split, which is by organization."},"caveats":["In nominal dollars payments grew 3.1 times over the same period; the real (inflation-adjusted) change is shown."],"explore":{"view":"np-flows","params":{"years":"2016-2025","area":"homeless"},"label":"See payments by service area"},"departments":["HOM","DPH","DEC","HSA"],"tags":["growth","homelessness","service areas"]},{"id":"voter-funds-paid-for-growth","rank":2,"theme":"spending-growth","title":"Voter-dedicated funds paid for 44% of nonprofit growth since FY2019; the General Fund 40%","dek":"Payments to nonprofits rose $925 million from FY2019 to FY2025. The Our City Our Home homelessness fund went from nothing to $235 million, the Children and Families Fund from $21 million to $158 million, while General Fund payments grew 61% and fell from 64% to 52% of the total.","why_it_matters":"Money from dedicated funds can only be spent on the purposes voters set, so a growing share of the nonprofit budget is protected from ordinary budget trade-offs, and also tied to the tax revenue behind each fund. That shapes what can be cut or expanded when the budget tightens.","surprise":"The pandemic is often cited for the growth, but federal COVID-19 stimulus funds paid nonprofits at most $22 million in a year (FY2022).","numbers":[{"label":"Increase in payments to nonprofits, FY2019 → FY2025 (nominal)","value":"$925 million","source":"nonprofits/data/raw/insights/payments_fund_fy2019.csv … payments_fund_fy2026.csv#fund (DataSF n9pm-xkyq by fund)"},{"label":"Our City Our Home Fund (homelessness gross receipts tax), FY2019 → FY2025","value":"$0 → $235 million (25% of the increase)","source":"nonprofits/data/raw/insights/payments_fund_fy2019.csv … payments_fund_fy2026.csv#fund (DataSF n9pm-xkyq by fund) (funds named Our City Our Home / OCOH)"},{"label":"Children and Families Fund, FY2019 → FY2025","value":"$21 million → $158 million (15% of the increase)","source":"nonprofits/data/raw/insights/payments_fund_fy2019.csv … payments_fund_fy2026.csv#fund (DataSF n9pm-xkyq by fund)"},{"label":"Children's Fund and Dignity Fund (seniors and adults with disabilities)","value":"$126 million → $163 million (4%)","source":"nonprofits/data/raw/insights/payments_fund_fy2019.csv … payments_fund_fy2026.csv#fund (DataSF n9pm-xkyq by fund)"},{"label":"General Fund, FY2019 → FY2025","value":"$610 million → $981 million (40% of the increase)","source":"nonprofits/data/raw/insights/payments_fund_fy2019.csv … payments_fund_fy2026.csv#fund (DataSF n9pm-xkyq by fund) (fund type GEN_FUND)"}],"chart":{"type":"stacked","title":"New dedicated funds carried much of the growth","subtitle":"City payments to nonprofits by fund, millions of nominal dollars","x":{"label":"Fiscal year","values":[2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025,2026]},"y":{"label":"Millions of dollars","unit":"$M"},"series":[{"name":"General Fund","values":[609.68,683.78,819.92,913.56,976.3,998.3,981.11,934.85]},{"name":"Our City Our Home","values":[0.0,3.53,16.6,54.93,121.4,147.39,234.67,217.39]},{"name":"Children and Families Fund","values":[21.34,30.13,50.64,68.04,157.73,138.68,158.37,199.11]},{"name":"Children's Fund and Dignity Fund","values":[126.22,144.95,147.61,156.04,190.13,189.52,163.46,203.78]},{"name":"Grants and other funds","values":[197.57,204.21,230.07,219.82,244.8,291.79,342.41,341.27]}],"highlight":"Our City Our Home","note":"Source: Vendor Payments (DataSF n9pm-xkyq) aggregated by fund for organizations matched to IRS exempt-organization records; totals equal the dashboard's. \"Grants and other funds\" are state and federal grant funds, the hotel-tax arts fund, the opioid settlement fund and other special revenue and enterprise funds. Fiscal years run July to June and are labeled by the year they end."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held in real dollars: dedicated funds 52% of the real increase, the General Fund 34%.","Held to FY2026: dedicated funds 50% of the increase since FY2019, the General Fund 35%.","Held as a reconciliation check: fund-level payments add up to the dashboard total (FY2025 within 2 dollars).","Held when checking where Our City Our Home money goes: 63% of it was paid by the homelessness department (HSH) in FY2025."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Fund names describe the account the city charged, not every revenue source behind it; the Children and Families Fund also holds state money and existed before FY2019. Starting in FY2019 avoids the older accounting system, whose fund names differ."},"caveats":["Nominal dollars: the fund mix is an accounting identity in the year of payment.","Payments from Our City Our Home (the November 2018 Proposition C business tax for homelessness) begin in FY2020 and scale up from FY2022."],"explore":{"view":"np-flows","params":{"years":"2019-2025","dept":"HOM"},"label":"See HSH's nonprofit payments"},"departments":["HOM","DEC","CHF","HSA"],"tags":["funding","Our City Our Home","set-asides"]},{"id":"growth-went-to-existing-partners","rank":3,"theme":"spending-growth","title":"81% of the growth went to nonprofits the city already paid in FY2016","dek":"The 643 organizations paid in both FY2016 and FY2025 received $869 million more in real terms, a 112% increase; the 321 organizations new since FY2016 received $236 million in FY2025. Each year, first-time recipients got on average 1.2% of the dollars.","why_it_matters":"The city mostly expanded existing relationships rather than bringing in new providers. That can mean continuity for clients and proven partners, and it also means that competition for new money is largely among incumbents.","surprise":"The growth was concentrated at the top: the typical continuing organization grew only 28% after inflation, while the 20 largest recipients of FY2025 alone account for 54% of the total increase.","numbers":[{"label":"Real increase at organizations paid in both FY2016 and FY2025","value":"$869 million (643 organizations, +112%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator)"},{"label":"FY2025 payments to organizations not paid in FY2016","value":"$236 million (321 organizations)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator)"},{"label":"FY2016 payments to organizations not paid in FY2025","value":"$34 million (205 organizations)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator)"},{"label":"Share of each year's dollars going to first-time recipients, FY2008–FY2026 average","value":"1.2% (highest 3.9%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"}],"chart":{"type":"waterfall","title":"Existing partners took most of the new money","subtitle":"City payments to nonprofits, FY2016 to FY2025, millions of FY2025 dollars","x":{"label":"Component","values":["FY2016 total","Organizations that stopped","Growth at continuing organizations","Organizations new since FY2016","FY2025 total"]},"y":{"label":"Millions of FY2025 dollars","unit":"$M"},"series":[{"name":"Payments","values":[809.62,-34.35,868.59,236.18,1880.03]}],"highlight":"Growth at continuing organizations","note":"Source: city Vendor Payments (DataSF n9pm-xkyq), expenditure payments to organizations matched to IRS exempt-organization records. \"Continuing\" = paid in both FY2016 and FY2025. Real dollars are FY2025 dollars (SF-Oakland-Hayward CPI-U, BLS CUURS49BSA0)."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held from FY2019: continuing organizations took 75% of the growth.","Held counting as new only organizations never paid from FY2007 to FY2016: they account for 21% of the growth.","Held excluding organizations that mostly pass money on: continuing organizations 82% of the growth.","Held at the median: the typical organization's real payment rose from $87,000 to $116,000."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Organizations that merged or changed their tax ID look like an exit plus an entry. A new organization may also run a program that an older one ran before, so \"new\" does not always mean a new program."},"caveats":["Counts organizations, not programs; a continuing organization may have won new contracts."],"explore":{"view":"np-orgs","params":{"years":"2016-2025"},"label":"See every organization paid"},"departments":[],"tags":["growth","incumbents","new providers"]},{"id":"pandemic-step-up-stayed","rank":4,"theme":"spending-growth","title":"The pandemic-era jump never unwound: real payments rose 63% FY2019–FY2025","dek":"Real payments to nonprofits rose 27% from FY2019 to FY2021 and another 29% from FY2021 to FY2025, to $1.88 billion. They slipped only 2% in FY2026, even as the number of organizations paid rose just from 907 to 964.","why_it_matters":"Spending that began as an emergency response became the baseline, much of it funded by new dedicated taxes rather than one-time relief. With city revenues under pressure, whether that level can be sustained is a live budget question.","surprise":"One might expect emergency spending to recede after the pandemic; instead growth after FY2021 was as large as during it.","numbers":[{"label":"Real payments FY2019 → FY2021 → FY2025","value":"$1.15 billion → $1.46 billion → $1.88 billion","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Change FY2025 → FY2026 (real)","value":"−2%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Organizations paid, FY2019 → FY2025","value":"907 → 964","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Largest year of federal COVID-19 stimulus fund payments to nonprofits","value":"$22 million (FY2022)","source":"nonprofits/data/raw/insights/payments_fund_fy2019.csv … payments_fund_fy2026.csv#fund (DataSF n9pm-xkyq by fund)"}],"chart":{"type":"line","title":"No post-pandemic retreat","subtitle":"City payments to nonprofits, millions of FY2025 dollars","x":{"label":"Fiscal year","values":[2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025,2026]},"y":{"label":"Millions of FY2025 dollars","unit":"$M"},"series":[{"name":"Payments (real)","values":[721.03,715.78,728.03,701.26,710.33,685.73,705.62,722.44,769.93,809.62,959.67,872.98,1153.99,1260.16,1462.08,1557.91,1779.15,1803.75,1880.03,1836.16]}],"annotations":[{"x":2018,"label":"Accounting change"},{"x":2020,"label":"COVID-19"}],"note":"Source: city Vendor Payments (DataSF n9pm-xkyq), expenditure payments to organizations matched to IRS exempt-organization records. FY2018 is low partly because of the switch to a new accounting system. Real dollars are FY2025 dollars (SF-Oakland-Hayward CPI-U, BLS CUURS49BSA0)."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held in the Controller's own nonprofit spending dataset (qkex-vh98): nominal growth FY2019–FY2025 101%, against 97% here.","Held excluding organizations that mostly pass money on: real growth FY2019–FY2025 60%.","Held for the typical organization: the median real payment rose from $87,000 in FY2019 to $116,000 in FY2025."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"FY2026 payments could still rise slightly as late invoices post. The consumer price index is an imperfect deflator for wage-heavy nonprofit budgets; the U.S. services index gives somewhat lower real growth (red team)."},"caveats":["FY2019 is used as the pre-pandemic year because FY2018 is distorted by the accounting-system change."],"explore":{"view":"np-overview","params":{"years":"2019-2026"},"label":"See the payment trend"},"departments":[],"tags":["pandemic","baseline","trend"]},{"id":"dependency-held-steady","rank":5,"theme":"financial-health","title":"City money tripled, but the median large recipient still gets 45% of revenue from it","dek":"Among organizations paid at least $1 million, the median got 45% of its revenue from the city in FY2016 and 45% in FY2024. For the 102 organizations in both years, city payments rose 151% (nominal) but the median share moved only from 46% to 47%.","why_it_matters":"The growth did not simply make existing nonprofits more reliant on the city: their other revenue grew about as fast. The number of large, highly dependent organizations still rose, because more organizations crossed the $1 million line.","surprise":"With city payments tripling, a reader would expect reliance on the city to climb steeply; the median barely moved.","numbers":[{"label":"Median city share of revenue, organizations paid ≥ $1 million, FY2016 → FY2024","value":"45% → 45% (109 → 199 organizations)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Share of those organizations above 75% dependent","value":"18% → 21%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Same organizations in both years: median share","value":"46% → 47% (102 organizations)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"}],"chart":{"type":"line","title":"Reliance on the city has stayed in a narrow band","subtitle":"Organizations paid at least $1 million in the year: median city share of revenue, and share above 75% dependent (%)","x":{"label":"Fiscal year","values":[2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025]},"y":{"label":"Percent","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Median city share of revenue","values":[36.58,41.46,39.51,44.67,44.79,48.33,38.09,41.85,41.19,39.08,44.17,48.04,44.87,46.35]},{"name":"Share of organizations above 75% dependent","values":[16.85,19.35,19.19,18.87,18.35,24.35,12.8,21.68,20.75,21.12,22.47,23.78,20.6,24.35]}],"highlight":"Median city share of revenue","note":"City dependency = city payments in the fiscal year divided by total revenue on the organization's Form 990 for the matching year. Source: city Vendor Payments and IRS Form 990 (e-file and ProPublica). Fiscal years run July to June and are labeled by the year they end."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held for the same organizations in both years: median 46% → 47%.","Held with a $1 million threshold in real dollars: median 42% in FY2016 and 45% in FY2024.","Held with city payments prorated to each organization's own tax year (red team): FY2024 median 45%.","Held in FY2025: median 46%; every year from FY2012 to FY2025 falls between 37% and 48%."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Organizations whose tax year does not end in June are compared with the city fiscal year that ends in the same calendar year, which blurs single years. Money the city passes through other nonprofits shows up as non-city revenue."},"caveats":["Total revenue on the Form 990 includes government money from all sources; \"city share\" counts only direct city payments."],"explore":{"view":"np-dependency","params":{"years":"2016-2024"},"label":"See city dependency by organization"},"departments":[],"tags":["dependency","Form 990"]},{"id":"dependency-and-corrective-action","rank":6,"theme":"financial-health","title":"Nonprofits over 75% city-funded were 4.1 times as likely to be on corrective action or worse","dek":"In the Controller's monitoring for FY2023–FY2025, 13% of organization-years with more than 75% of revenue from the city ended on corrective action, elevated concern, Tier 3 or red-flag status, against 3.2% for the rest. Counting each organization once, it was 13 of 44 against 9 of 199.","why_it_matters":"If heavy reliance on the city goes with weaker finances or administration, monitoring and technical assistance can be aimed where risk is highest. The pattern is an association: it does not show that city funding causes problems, and it rests on few cases.","surprise":null,"numbers":[{"label":"Severe monitoring status, org-years above 75% city-funded","value":"16 of 121 (13%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/reviews/findings.json#result; nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Severe monitoring status, all other org-years","value":"15 of 466 (3.2%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/reviews/findings.json#result; nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Organizations counted once: severe status at least once","value":"13 of 44 vs 9 of 199","source":"nonprofits/data/site/reviews/findings.json#result"},{"label":"Adjusted odds ratio (size, service area, year), 95% interval","value":"4.9 (1.9 to 12.4)","source":"nonprofits/data/redteam/redteam.json#dependency_corrective_action"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"The more city-dependent, the more often on corrective action","subtitle":"Share of organization-years ending on corrective action, elevated concern, Tier 3 or red flag, by city share of revenue, FY2023–FY2025 (%)","x":{"label":"City share of revenue","values":["Under 25%","25–50%","50–75%","75% or more"]},"y":{"label":"Percent of organization-years","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Severe monitoring status","values":[1.25,2.98,5.8,13.22],"n":[160,168,138,121]}],"highlight":"75% or more","note":"Source: Controller, Citywide Nonprofit Monitoring and Capacity Building Program annual reports FY2023–FY2025 (per-organization appendices); City dependency = city payments in the fiscal year divided by total revenue on the organization's Form 990 for the matching year. Monitoring checks fiscal and administrative standards; a designation is not a finding of misuse of funds."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held after controlling for size, service area and year (red team): odds ratio 4.9, 95% interval 1.9 to 12.4; 4.2 when also controlling for thin reserves.","Held when shuffling dependency labels between organizations (permutation test): p = 0.002.","Held within size bands, service areas and years (Mantel-Haenszel risk ratios at least 4.0).","Did not hold clearly in each organization's first monitored year only: 2 vs 3 severe cases, too few to tell."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The pattern rests on 31 severe org-years at 22 organizations, many of them repeat designations of the same organization. Organizations that rely heavily on the city may also be the ones departments watch most closely."},"caveats":["The corrective-action tier policy began in FY2024; earlier years use elevated-concern and red-flag statuses.","Only organizations in the Controller's joint monitoring pool are included."],"explore":{"view":"np-reviews","params":{"years":"2023-2025"},"label":"See monitoring results"},"departments":[],"tags":["monitoring","corrective action","dependency"]},{"id":"doubled-contracts-mostly-got-longer","rank":7,"theme":"contracting","title":"Of 216 nonprofit contracts that doubled, only 35 doubled per year","dek":"Among 974 large nonprofit contracts, 216 at least doubled from their first observed amount. 211 of those had their end date pushed out by six months or more, and a Claude review of each record classed 162 as mainly longer terms and 44 as mainly larger yearly amounts.","why_it_matters":"A contract that \"doubled\" sounds like runaway cost growth, but most of these grew because the city kept the same provider for more years rather than because yearly spending doubled. The policy question is a different one: how often long-running services should be competed again.","surprise":"The headline doubling usually reflects time, not scale: the amount per year of term doubled in only 35 of the 216 cases.","numbers":[{"label":"Large nonprofit contracts that at least doubled since first observed","value":"216 of 974","source":"nonprofits/data/redteam/parts/doubled_contracts.json#term_extended_days,annualized_ratio; nonprofits/data/site/contracts/contracts.json"},{"label":"Of those, end date extended by six months or more","value":"211","source":"nonprofits/data/redteam/parts/doubled_contracts.json#term_extended_days,annualized_ratio"},{"label":"Of those, amount per year of term at least doubled","value":"35","source":"nonprofits/data/redteam/parts/doubled_contracts.json#term_extended_days,annualized_ratio"},{"label":"Classified mainly as a longer term / a larger yearly amount","value":"162 / 44","source":"nonprofits/data/redteam/parts/contracts_llm.json#primary_driver"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Why contracts doubled","subtitle":"Contracts of at least $4 million that doubled since first observed, by main driver (count)","x":{"label":"Main driver","values":["Mainly a longer term","Mainly a larger yearly amount","Funding for years already in the term","Higher rates or volume"]},"y":{"label":"Contracts","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"Contracts","values":[162,44,8,2]}],"highlight":"Mainly a longer term","note":"Source: Supplier Contracts (DataSF cqi5-hm2d) snapshots from 2021, 2022, 2025 and 2026, with vendor payments per contract. Drivers were classified by Claude (Opus, high effort) from each contract's term history, per-year amounts and payments; the red team reviewed the method."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held without the model: 211 of 216 had the end date pushed out, and 151 grew less than 1.5 times per year of term.","Held among confident classifications (at least 0.7): 141 of 155 mainly longer terms.","Did not fully hold on actual spending: yearly payments rose 1.5 times or more (nominal) on 102 of the 172 contracts with a payment history, so many longer contracts also spent more per year."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Contracts first observed in 2022 or 2025 may have been amended before then, which the snapshots cannot see. Short title fields rarely show whether scope changed (212 of 216 cannot tell)."},"caveats":["\"Doubled\" compares the current amount with the first snapshot, not with the original signed amount."],"explore":{"view":"np-growth","params":{},"label":"See contract growth by contract"},"departments":["DPH","HOM","HSA"],"tags":["contracts","amendments","extensions"]},{"id":"homelessness-spending-and-the-count","rank":8,"theme":"outcomes","title":"HSH nonprofit spending rose 143%; unsheltered count fell 34%, total barely moved","dek":"Real payments by the homelessness department (HSH) to nonprofits rose from $207 million in FY2019 to $504 million in FY2026. Between the January 2019 and 2026 counts, people counted unsheltered fell from 5,180 to 3,400 and people in shelter rose from 2,855 to 4,573, while the total went from 8,035 to 7,973.","why_it_matters":"The pattern fits spending that moved people off the street into shelter and housing while new people kept becoming homeless. Whether the next dollar should go to shelter, housing or prevention depends on which of those effects it buys.","surprise":"More than doubling spending did not shrink the total count, but the split between street and shelter changed sharply.","numbers":[{"label":"HSH payments to nonprofits, real, FY2019 → FY2026","value":"$207 million → $504 million (+143%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt (dept HOM)"},{"label":"Unsheltered, January count 2019 → 2026","value":"5,180 → 3,400 (−34%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/aggregates/outcomes.json#value (pit_unsheltered)"},{"label":"Sheltered (total minus unsheltered)","value":"2,855 → 4,573 (+60%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/aggregates/outcomes.json#value (pit_total, pit_unsheltered)"},{"label":"Total homeless count","value":"8,035 → 7,973 (−1%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/aggregates/outcomes.json#value (pit_total)"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"More spending, fewer people outside, same total","subtitle":"Percent change, FY2019 to FY2026 (spending, real dollars) and January 2019 to January 2026 (Point-in-Time counts)","x":{"label":"Measure","values":["HSH payments to nonprofits (real)","People in shelter","Total homeless count","People unsheltered"]},"y":{"label":"Percent change","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Change","values":[143.3,60.18,-0.77,-34.36]}],"highlight":"People unsheltered","note":"Sources: city Vendor Payments (department HOM); Point-in-Time counts from HSH and the Controller (DataSF kc49-udxn, measure 7169) and the preliminary 2026 results on sf.gov. Real dollars are FY2025 dollars (SF-Oakland-Hayward CPI-U, BLS CUURS49BSA0)."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held with the final 2024 count instead of the preliminary 2026 one: unsheltered −16%, total +4%, spending FY2019–FY2024 +100%.","Held counting all homelessness-area organizations, whichever department paid them: real spending +124%.","Held in nominal dollars: HSH payments +204%."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The 2026 figures are preliminary and the unsheltered number is rounded. Point-in-Time counts are single January nights and depend on methods and weather; they measure the population, not the effect of any one program."},"caveats":["No causal claim: regional housing costs, the economy and inflows from other counties also drive the count.","Spending also pays for permanent supportive housing for formerly homeless people, who are not in the count at all."],"explore":{"view":"np-flows","params":{"years":"2019-2026","dept":"HOM"},"label":"See HSH's payments to nonprofits"},"departments":["HOM"],"tags":["homelessness","outcomes","Point-in-Time count"]},{"id":"dependent-nonprofits-thin-reserves","rank":9,"theme":"financial-health","title":"27% of heavily city-funded nonprofits had under a month of reserves, vs 8%","dek":"In FY2024, 27% of large recipients getting at least 75% of revenue from the city had less than one month of unrestricted net assets, against 8% of the others; their median cushion was 3.9 months against 5.4.","why_it_matters":"Organizations with thin reserves depend on the city paying on time; a late payment or a delayed contract can force layoffs or borrowing.","surprise":"Heavily city-funded organizations were no more likely to run a deficit (35% vs 35%); the difference is in the cushion, not the annual result.","numbers":[{"label":"Under one month of reserves, organizations ≥ 75% city-funded (FY2024)","value":"27% (37 organizations)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2024.json#total_revenue,months_reserves; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Under one month of reserves, other organizations paid ≥ $1 million","value":"8% (156 organizations)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2024.json#total_revenue,months_reserves; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Median months of unrestricted reserves","value":"3.9 vs 5.4","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2024.json#total_revenue,months_reserves"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"Thin cushions cluster among the most city-dependent","subtitle":"Organizations paid at least $1 million in FY2024: share with under one month of unrestricted reserves, by city share of revenue (%)","x":{"label":"City share of revenue","values":["Under 25%","25–50%","50–75%","75% or more"]},"y":{"label":"Percent of organizations","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Under one month of reserves","values":[5.56,5.17,15.91,27.03],"n":[54,58,44,37]}],"highlight":"75% or more","note":"Months of reserves = unrestricted net assets divided by one month of expenses, from the Form 990. City dependency = city payments in the fiscal year divided by total revenue on the organization's Form 990 for the matching year."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held in FY2023: 21% vs 8%.","Held in FY2025: 20% vs 6%.","Held excluding pass-through organizations and dependency above 100%: 26% vs 7%.","Held with a two-month threshold: 32% vs 19%."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Unrestricted net assets include buildings and equipment, so they overstate cash for property-owning organizations and the comparison is rough. Only 37 organizations are in the high-dependency group."},"caveats":["Form 990 year-end figures are a snapshot; reserves can change quickly."],"explore":{"view":"np-pay","params":{"years":"2024"},"label":"See reserves by organization"},"departments":[],"tags":["reserves","dependency","Form 990"]},{"id":"emergency-authority-contracts","rank":10,"theme":"contracting","title":"Emergency or bid-waiver authority covered 38% of contract dollars starting in FY2021","dek":"Of $19.7 billion in current nonprofit contract amounts, $2.71 billion (14%) was awarded under emergency procurement or ordinances that waived competitive solicitation, 87% of it at the homelessness department. The share rose from 1.4% of contracts starting in FY2019 to 38% in FY2021 and was still 15% in FY2025.","why_it_matters":"These authorities come from city law and from ordinances the Board of Supervisors passed to speed up shelter and behavioral-health services. Faster contracting can cost competition and price discovery, so how long such authority stays in place is a fair question.","surprise":"The share fell after the pandemic but did not return to pre-2020 levels: 73% of such dollars in contracts starting FY2024 or later used the 2023 ordinance on homelessness, overdose and behavioral-health contracting.","numbers":[{"label":"Contract amounts under emergency or waiver authority (current awarded amount)","value":"$2.71 billion of $19.7 billion (14%)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/contracts/all_1.json … all_5.json#authority_class,agreed,start"},{"label":"Share of that at the homelessness department","value":"87%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/contracts/all_1.json … all_5.json#authority_class,agreed,start"},{"label":"Share of dollars in contracts starting FY2019 / FY2021 / FY2025","value":"1.4% / 38% / 15%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/contracts/all_1.json … all_5.json#authority_class,agreed,start"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"Emergency contracting surged and has not gone away","subtitle":"Share of nonprofit contract dollars awarded under emergency or bid-waiver authority, by fiscal year the contract started (%)","x":{"label":"Fiscal year the contract started","values":[2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025,2026]},"y":{"label":"Percent of contract dollars","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Emergency or waiver authority","values":[0.78,1.45,17.39,37.5,32.78,15.85,10.15,14.78,11.33]}],"highlight":"2021","note":"Source: Supplier Contracts (DataSF cqi5-hm2d), prime contracts of at least $250,000 with matched nonprofits. Authority classes from the contracts' purchasing-authority text; emergency or waiver includes Chapter 21 emergency procurement and ordinances that waived solicitation."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held counting contracts rather than dollars: 11% of contracts starting in FY2021, 6% in FY2025.","Held for contracts of at least $1 million: 39% of FY2021 dollars.","Held without the five largest FY2021 contracts: 26%.","Did not hold outside the homelessness department: only 4.6% of other departments' FY2021 contract dollars."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Contract amounts are current ceilings, not spending, and the authority text is how the city recorded it; a few contracts may have been competed informally even under a waiver."},"caveats":["Using emergency or waiver authority is legal when authorized; it is unusual, not improper."],"explore":{"view":"np-growth","params":{"dept":"HOM"},"label":"See HSH's contracts"},"departments":["HOM","DPH"],"tags":["contracts","emergency procurement","competition"]},{"id":"top-recipients-reshuffled","rank":11,"theme":"concentration","title":"The top 20 still get 45% of dollars, but only 9 of FY2016's top 20 remain","dek":"The 20 largest recipients have received between 41% and 47% of nonprofit dollars every year since FY2008. Membership turned over faster after FY2018: about 4.0 new names a year, against 1.4 a year from FY2009 to FY2017.","why_it_matters":"Concentration is steady, but the list is not a closed club: organizations such as Five Keys Schools and Programs, Homebridge and the SF Housing Accelerator Fund went from under $1 million in FY2016 to the top 20.","surprise":"Only 7 organizations have been in the top 20 every year since FY2007.","numbers":[{"label":"Top-20 share of nonprofit dollars, FY2016 → FY2025","value":"45% → 45%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"FY2016 top-20 organizations still in the FY2025 top 20","value":"9","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator)"},{"label":"New names in the top 20 per year, FY2009–FY2017 vs FY2019–FY2026","value":"1.4 vs 4.0","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"FY2025 top-20 members paid under $1 million in FY2016","value":"5","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator)"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"More turnover at the top since FY2018","subtitle":"Number of organizations entering the top 20 recipients each fiscal year","x":{"label":"Fiscal year","values":[2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025,2026]},"y":{"label":"New names in the top 20","unit":"count"},"series":[{"name":"New names","values":[1,3,4,1,0,0,0,1,2,2,3,3,3,4,3,5,4,5,5]}],"annotations":[{"x":2018,"label":"Accounting change"}],"note":"Source: city Vendor Payments (DataSF n9pm-xkyq), expenditure payments to organizations matched to IRS exempt-organization records. Ranked by nominal payments within each year."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held for the top 50: 4.2 new names a year before FY2018 vs 5.5 after.","Held excluding pass-through organizations: 1.3 vs 3.9 new names a year.","Held for concentration: the top-20 share stayed between 41% and 47% in every year FY2008–FY2026."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Reorganizations and new departments move contracts between providers in bulk (for example childcare subsidies and shelter operations), so some turnover reflects a few large program transfers rather than broad competition."},"caveats":["Ranks are by city payments, not by organization size."],"explore":{"view":"np-flows","params":{"years":"2016-2025"},"label":"See the largest recipients"},"departments":[],"tags":["concentration","top recipients","turnover"]},{"id":"pass-through-organizations","rank":12,"theme":"concentration","title":"About $1 in 7 goes to groups that mostly pass money on, led by childcare subsidies","dek":"In FY2025, $277 million (15%) went to 11 organizations that mainly pass city money to families, borrowers or projects: childcare-subsidy administrators, a housing loan fund and fiscal sponsors. Children's Council of San Francisco alone received $149 million, up from $22 million in FY2016.","why_it_matters":"Counting these dollars as \"nonprofit spending\" overstates what nonprofits themselves spend on staff and services, and makes the largest recipient look like the largest provider. Excluding them, real growth since FY2016 is 113% rather than 132%.","surprise":"The city's largest nonprofit recipient mostly pays childcare subsidies to providers and families rather than running programs itself.","numbers":[{"label":"FY2025 payments to pass-through and fiscal-sponsor organizations","value":"$277 million (15% of $1.88 billion)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt; nonprofits/data/redteam/redteam.json#total_fy2025"},{"label":"Children's Council of San Francisco, FY2016 → FY2025 (nominal)","value":"$22 million → $149 million","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2016.json; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2025.json#amt (deflated with nonprofits/data/site/meta.json#deflator)"},{"label":"Real growth FY2016 → FY2025, all vs excluding pass-through organizations","value":"132% vs 113%","source":"nonprofits/data/redteam/redteam.json#real_growth_fy2016_2025"},{"label":"Controller's own FY2025 nonprofit spending total (narrower definition)","value":"$1.63 billion","source":"nonprofits/data/redteam/redteam.json#total_fy2025 (DataSF qkex-vh98)"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"The largest pass-through recipients","subtitle":"FY2025 city payments, millions of dollars","x":{"label":"Organization","values":["Children's Council of SF","SF Housing Accelerator Fund","Wu Yee Children's Services","Tides Center","Heluna Health","San Francisco Study Center","SF Public Health Foundation"]},"y":{"label":"Millions of dollars","unit":"$M"},"series":[{"name":"FY2025 payments","values":[149.2,44.66,26.11,20.65,17.76,9.44,4.37]}],"highlight":"Children's Council of SF","note":"Source: city Vendor Payments (DataSF n9pm-xkyq), expenditure payments to organizations matched to IRS exempt-organization records. Pass-through list from the red team: childcare-subsidy administrators, a loan fund and fiscal sponsors or intermediaries."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held in FY2016 on the same list: 7% of dollars, so the share roughly doubled.","Held with the Controller's narrower definition, which excludes the main childcare-subsidy contracts: $1.63 billion in FY2025.","Held for loans: $50 million of FY2025 payments were city loans to nonprofits, mostly through the housing loan fund."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The list is a judgment: fiscal sponsors also run some programs directly, and some service providers regrant part of their funds. The share depends on which organizations are included."},"caveats":["Pass-through is a description of the funding role, not a criticism; administering subsidies is the service."],"explore":{"view":"org","params":{"id":"org-942221305"},"label":"See Children's Council's profile"},"departments":["DEC","HSA","MYR"],"tags":["pass-through","childcare","fiscal sponsors"]},{"id":"monitoring-findings-returned-to-normal","rank":13,"theme":"measurement","title":"Monitoring findings tripled after FY2022, a return to pre-pandemic rates","dek":"The share of monitored nonprofits ending the Controller's review with unresolved findings or worse rose from 7.3% in FY2022 to 24% in FY2023 and 26% in FY2025. In FY2019, before the pandemic, it was 24%.","why_it_matters":"Read naively, the jump suggests nonprofits got worse at managing money. The Controller attributes it to the end of pandemic-era discretionary and streamlined monitoring, fewer good-performance waivers and more consistent monitors, so FY2022 is the outlier.","surprise":"Among the 159 organizations monitored in both FY2022 and FY2023, the share with findings rose from 6% to 20%, so a change in who was monitored does not explain the jump.","numbers":[{"label":"Unresolved findings or worse at final status, FY2022 → FY2023 → FY2025","value":"7.3% → 24% → 26%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/reviews/findings.json#result"},{"label":"Not in full conformance at final status, FY2019 (report text)","value":"24%","source":"nonprofits/data/reviews/text/monitoring_FY23_annual_report.txt (\"In FY19, … ended the cycle in full conformance\")"},{"label":"Good-performance waivers granted, FY2022 → FY2023 → FY2024","value":"39 → 18 → 12","source":"nonprofits/data/reviews/text/monitoring_FY22_annual_report.txt; nonprofits/data/reviews/text/monitoring_FY23_annual_report.txt; nonprofits/data/reviews/text/monitoring_FY24_annual_report.txt"},{"label":"Corrective action, elevated concern, Tier 3 or red flag, FY2022 → FY2025","value":"4 → 16","source":"nonprofits/data/site/reviews/findings.json#result"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"FY2022 was the outlier","subtitle":"Share of monitored nonprofits ending the review with unresolved findings or a more severe status (%)","x":{"label":"Monitoring year","values":[2019,2022,2023,2024,2025]},"y":{"label":"Percent of monitored nonprofits","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Unresolved findings or worse","values":[24.0,7.34,23.96,28.85,25.51]}],"highlight":"2022","note":"Source: Controller, Citywide Nonprofit Monitoring and Capacity Building Program annual reports; FY2019 from the FY2023 report's comparison; FY2020 and FY2021 were not published. Fiscal years run July to June and are labeled by the year they end."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held for the same organizations: 6% → 20% among 159 monitored both years.","Held against the report's own count: 24% with unresolved findings in FY2023.","Held in the report narrative: FY2023 conformance was the same as pre-COVID years; waivers fell from 39 to 12."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Standards also changed (for example the operating-cash test was halved in FY2024), so years are not strictly comparable. Severe designations rose too (4 to 16), partly because the tier policy only began in FY2024."},"caveats":["A monitoring finding concerns fiscal and administrative standards; it is not evidence of misuse of funds."],"explore":{"view":"np-reviews","params":{},"label":"See monitoring results by year"},"departments":["CON"],"tags":["monitoring","measurement","Controller"]},{"id":"executive-pay-below-peers","rank":14,"theme":"staffing","title":"Top pay at the most city-dependent nonprofits runs 29% below same-size peers","dek":"Comparing each organization's highest-paid officer with others of the same budget size in FY2024, organizations getting at least 75% of revenue from the city paid a median 29% less than expected; those under 25% city-funded were at +3%. Median top pay was $178,000 against $271,000.","why_it_matters":"Concern about executive pay at city-funded nonprofits is common. On this evidence, the organizations that rely most on the city pay their leaders less than organizations of the same size, not more, which may matter for recruiting and retention.","surprise":null,"numbers":[{"label":"Median pay gap vs same-size organizations, ≥ 75% city-funded (FY2024)","value":"−29% (34 organizations)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp#exec_comp,total_expenses"},{"label":"Median pay gap, under 25% city-funded","value":"+3%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp#exec_comp,total_expenses"},{"label":"Pay rises with size: elasticity of top pay to expenses","value":"0.39 (589 organizations)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp#exec_comp,total_expenses"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"More city-dependent, lower pay for size","subtitle":"Median gap between top-officer pay and the pay expected for an organization of the same expense size, FY2024 (%)","x":{"label":"City share of revenue","values":["Under 25%","25–50%","50–75%","75% or more"]},"y":{"label":"Percent above or below expected","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Pay gap","values":[3.26,-14.13,-24.45,-29.04],"n":[430,74,51,34]}],"highlight":"75% or more","note":"Organizations with at least $1 million in expenses and any city payment; expected pay from a log-log fit of top-officer pay (Form 990 Part VII, all compensation) on total expenses across the same organizations. City dependency = city payments in the fiscal year divided by total revenue on the organization's Form 990 for the matching year."},"robustness":{"verdict":"fragile","checks":["Held in other years: −35% (FY2022), −26% (FY2023), −27% (FY2025).","Held with service-area controls: −30% for the most dependent group.","Did not hold at full strength when the peer group is limited to organizations paid at least $1 million by the city: −9%, still below but much smaller."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"The peer group matters: the wider comparison includes hospitals, universities and museums that pay more at any size. The Form 990 does not say whether the top officer worked full time or the full year."},"caveats":["Compares organizations, not individuals; no executive is singled out.","Sector pay norms here come from the city's own nonprofit vendors, not a national survey."],"explore":{"view":"np-pay","params":{"years":"2024"},"label":"See executive pay against size"},"departments":[],"tags":["executive pay","Form 990","dependency"]},{"id":"spending-outpaced-people-served","rank":15,"theme":"outcomes","title":"Where measures are consistent, real spending rose 11%–23% while people served stalled","dek":"In five services with a consistent published measure, real payments to nonprofits by the department rose while the count served fell or barely changed: children and youth served by DCYF −24% (spending +16%), DPH mental health clients −2% (+23%), households HSH placed in housing −13% (+22%).","why_it_matters":"Spending per person served rose in all 5 comparisons. That can mean higher wages, deeper services or costlier clients, all defensible, but it is the question budget hearings should ask of each department.","surprise":null,"numbers":[{"label":"DCYF: children and youth served vs real nonprofit payments, FY2019 → FY2025","value":"−24% vs +16%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/aggregates/outcomes.json#value; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"DPH: unique mental health clients vs payments, FY2020 → FY2025","value":"−2% vs +23%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/aggregates/outcomes.json#value; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"DPH: unique substance use clients vs payments, FY2020 → FY2025","value":"+5% vs +23%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/aggregates/outcomes.json#value; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"HSH: households placed vs payments, FY2023 → FY2025","value":"−13% vs +22%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/aggregates/outcomes.json#value; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"HSA: home-delivered meals vs payments, FY2018 → FY2025","value":"−1% vs +11%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/aggregates/outcomes.json#value; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"Spending up, counts flat or down","subtitle":"Percent change in the department's real payments to nonprofits and in the published count served, over each measure's consistent years","x":{"label":"Measure","values":["Children and youth served (DCYF)","Mental health clients (DPH)","Substance use clients (DPH)","Households placed in housing (HSH)","Home-delivered meals, older adults (HSA)"]},"y":{"label":"Percent change","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Real payments to nonprofits","values":[15.81,23.49,23.49,22.4,10.56]},{"name":"Count served","values":[-24.48,-2.25,4.69,-13.19,-0.68]}],"highlight":"Count served","note":"Windows: DCYF FY2019–FY2025, DPH FY2020–FY2025, HSH FY2023–FY2025 (one consistent placement series), HSA FY2018–FY2025. Counts cover all providers, including city staff. Real dollars are FY2025 dollars (SF-Oakland-Hayward CPI-U, BLS CUURS49BSA0)."},"robustness":{"verdict":"fragile","checks":["Did not hold for DCYF from FY2022: spending −13%, served −10%, because DCYF payments fell in FY2025.","Held for DCYF to FY2024: spending +54%, served −12%.","Held for HSH to FY2026 (year to date): spending +26%, placements −8%."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Department payments cover far more than the measured service, and counts of unique clients say nothing about the intensity or quality of care. The pandemic depressed some counts in the base years and raised costs."},"caveats":["Descriptive only; no measure here isolates what nonprofit contracts bought."],"explore":{"view":"np-overview","params":{"years":"2019-2025"},"label":"See outcomes against payments"},"departments":["CHF","DPH","HOM","HSA"],"tags":["outcomes","cost per person","measures"]},{"id":"growth-rate-depends-on-base-year","rank":16,"theme":"measurement","title":"The real growth figure swings from 96% to 144% depending on the start year","dek":"Measured to FY2025, real growth in nonprofit payments is 144% from FY2015, 132% from FY2016 and 96% from FY2017. FY2017 was unusually high and FY2018 unusually low (−9%), around the city's switch to a new accounting system, before a +32% jump in FY2019.","why_it_matters":"Any single \"growth since year X\" figure for nonprofit spending can be picked to look large or modest. Comparisons across the FY2018 system change should use several base years or an average annual rate, about 9.8% a year after inflation since FY2016.","surprise":"Public Health's nonprofit payments dropped from $273 million to $173 million in FY2018 and recovered to $239 million in FY2019, a swing unlikely to reflect services.","numbers":[{"label":"Real growth to FY2025 from FY2015 / FY2016 / FY2017","value":"+144% / +132% / +96%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Real change FY2017 → FY2018 and FY2018 → FY2019","value":"−9% and +32%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Public Health payments to nonprofits FY2017 / FY2018 / FY2019 (nominal)","value":"$273 million / $173 million / $239 million","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt (dept DPH)"},{"label":"Average real growth per year, FY2016 → FY2025","value":"9.8%","source":"nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"}],"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"Pick a year, get a growth rate","subtitle":"Real growth in city payments to nonprofits to FY2025, by starting fiscal year (%)","x":{"label":"Starting year","values":["From FY2013","From FY2014","From FY2015","From FY2016","From FY2017","From FY2018","From FY2019"]},"y":{"label":"Percent growth to FY2025","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Real growth to FY2025","values":[166.44,160.23,144.18,132.21,95.9,115.36,62.92]}],"highlight":"From FY2016","note":"Source: city Vendor Payments (DataSF n9pm-xkyq), expenditure payments to organizations matched to IRS exempt-organization records. Real dollars are FY2025 dollars (SF-Oakland-Hayward CPI-U, BLS CUURS49BSA0)."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held over a longer window: 8.5% a year after inflation from FY2013 to FY2025.","Held in the red team's independent re-derivation from the raw payment files: 96% from FY2017.","Held with the U.S. CPI instead of the Bay Area CPI (red team): real growth from FY2016 134%."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Part of the FY2017–FY2019 swing may be real timing of contract payments rather than accounting; the data cannot separate them."},"caveats":["The dashboard's headline uses FY2016 as the base, matching the red team's review."],"explore":{"view":"np-overview","params":{"years":"2013-2025"},"label":"See the long-run trend"},"departments":["DPH"],"tags":["measurement","base year","accounting change"]},{"id":"fastest-rising-dependency","rank":17,"theme":"financial-health","title":"19 nonprofits went from under 25% to over 60% city-funded within five years","dek":"Children's Council of San Francisco went from 24% to 77% of revenue from the city between FY2014 and FY2019, as its city payments grew from $16 million to $66 million; Homebridge went from 0.3% to 78% between FY2013 and FY2018.","why_it_matters":"A quick shift to city funding changes an organization's risk profile: it needs the cash-flow and compliance capacity of a government contractor, and the city becomes exposed if that single provider struggles.","surprise":null,"numbers":[{"label":"Organizations (revenue ≥ $500,000) that went from under 25% to 60%–105% city-funded within five years, with ≥ $2 million from the city","value":"19","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Children's Council of San Francisco","value":"24% (FY2014) → 77% (FY2019)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Homebridge","value":"0.3% (FY2013) → 78% (FY2018)","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp; nonprofits/data/site/payments/cells_2007.json … cells_2026.json#amt"},{"label":"Of the 19, still at least 50% city-funded in their latest filing","value":"9","source":"nonprofits/data/site/finances/form990_2011.json … form990_2025.json#total_revenue,months_reserves,exec_comp"}],"chart":{"type":"slope","title":"From a minor funder to the main one","subtitle":"City share of revenue (%) in the starting year and when it first passed 60%, the six largest by city dollars (years differ by organization)","x":{"label":"Point","values":["Before","After"]},"y":{"label":"City share of revenue (%)","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Children's Council of SF","values":[23.93,77.48]},{"name":"Homebridge","values":[0.26,77.78]},{"name":"Community Arts Stabilization Trust","values":[0.0,61.37]},{"name":"Main Street Launch","values":[11.11,81.43]},{"name":"SF Housing Development Corp.","values":[19.38,69.34]},{"name":"Chinese Culture Foundation","values":[18.86,80.95]}],"note":"City dependency = city payments in the fiscal year divided by total revenue on the organization's Form 990 for the matching year. Source: city Vendor Payments and IRS Form 990."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held with a 50% end threshold: 28 organizations.","Held beyond a single filing: 13 of the 19 were at least 50% city-funded again in one of the next two years' filings.","Held for persistence: 9 of the 19 remain at least 50% city-funded in their latest filing."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"A one-year mismatch between the city fiscal year and a non-June tax year can inflate a single year's ratio; the threshold of 105% screens out the worst mismatches."},"caveats":["Rising dependency is a description of funding mix, not a judgment about the organization."],"explore":{"view":"np-dependency","params":{},"label":"See dependency by organization"},"departments":["DEC","HSA"],"tags":["dependency","organizations"]},{"id":"subcontracting-is-invisible","rank":18,"theme":"measurement","title":"The city's contract data list subcontractors on 8 of 12,740 nonprofit contracts","dek":"Supplier Contracts records subcontractors on 19% of for-profit professional-services contracts but on 0.5% of nonprofit ones, and on 1 of the 14,654 grant agreements for any recipient; grants are 87% of nonprofit contracts.","why_it_matters":"When a funded nonprofit passes work to other organizations, the public record does not show it; the only systematic check is the Controller's \"Subcontracts\" monitoring standard. Tracing where money ends up requires the organizations' own filings.","surprise":"The gap is mostly about contract type: grant agreements almost never list subcontractors, whoever the recipient is.","numbers":[{"label":"Nonprofit prime contracts with any subcontractor listed","value":"8 of 12,740","source":"nonprofits/data/raw/supplier_contracts.csv (DataSF cqi5-hm2d)#Supplier Type,Non-Profit,Contract Type"},{"label":"Professional-services contracts with subcontractors listed, for-profit vs nonprofit primes","value":"19% vs 0.5%","source":"nonprofits/data/raw/supplier_contracts.csv (DataSF cqi5-hm2d)#Supplier Type,Non-Profit,Contract Type"},{"label":"Grant agreements with subcontractors listed (all recipients)","value":"1 of 14,654","source":"nonprofits/data/raw/supplier_contracts.csv (DataSF cqi5-hm2d)#Supplier Type,Non-Profit,Contract Type"},{"label":"Subcontract findings in Controller monitoring, FY2022–FY2025","value":"11","source":"nonprofits/data/site/reviews/findings.json#result#categories"}],"chart":{"type":"hbar","title":"Subcontracting shows up for vendors, not for grants","subtitle":"Share of prime contracts with at least one subcontractor listed in Supplier Contracts (%)","x":{"label":"Contract group","values":["For-profit professional services","Nonprofit professional services","Grant agreements (all recipients)"]},"y":{"label":"Percent of contracts","unit":"%"},"series":[{"name":"Subcontractor listed","values":[19.15,0.51,0.01],"n":[5237,980,14654]}],"highlight":"Nonprofit professional services","note":"Source: Supplier Contracts (DataSF cqi5-hm2d), current extract; nonprofit = the dataset's own Non-Profit flag."},"robustness":{"verdict":"robust","checks":["Held with this dashboard's matched organizations instead of the city's flag: 6 of 4,949 large nonprofit contracts list a subcontractor.","Held across all for-profit contract types: 11% list a subcontractor.","Held for grants to any recipient: 1 of 14,654."],"what_could_make_it_wrong":"Some nonprofits deliver all services with their own staff, so a missing listing is not proof of hidden subcontracting."},"caveats":["Form 990 Schedule I and Part VII show some regranting and large contractors, but only for the organization's whole budget."],"explore":{"view":"np-growth","params":{},"label":"See nonprofit contracts"},"departments":[],"tags":["subcontracting","transparency","contracts"]}]});
